FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:1741 — `product` preallocates `left.len().saturating_mul(right.len())` and then builds every pair, with no output-size cap and no `Error::Limit` guard anywhere in the arm (lines 1739–1753). Two lists of ~100k items (each buildable under the loop cap) make one builtin call try to allocate ~1e10 `Value`s; `saturating_mul` also turns an overflowing product into `usize::MAX`, which `Vec::with_capacity` rejects with a panic ("capacity overflow"). Because a builtin call is charged as a single step, the step/output limits never fire and the process aborts or OOMs instead of returning a Redblue error. This directly contradicts REPORT.md's `resource_limit` gate claim. Fix: bound the materialised product (e.g. refuse when `left.len() * right.len()` exceeds a stated maximum) and return `Error::Limit`; never derive `with_capacity` from an unchecked product.
2. [MAJOR] src/runtime.rs:1762 — `groupby` does `groups.iter_mut().find(|(key, _)| key == item)` once per input item, making it O(n²) inside a single, uncharged builtin call. A 200k-item list of distinct keys performs ~2e10 `Value` comparisons in one bytecode step; there is no iteration/time budget and no cancellation point, so the run hangs until the host is killed. This is the "unbounded loop / no cancellation" class and is also not covered by the REPORT's `resource_limit` claim (only cache eviction was tested). Fix: key groups with a deterministic map (insertion-ordered) or charge comparisons/steps and abort with `Error::Limit`.
3. [MINOR] src/parser.rs:1274 — the capacity guard `*value <= usize::MAX as f64` is unsound: on 64-bit `usize::MAX as f64` rounds up to 2^64, so `lru_cache(18446744073709551616)` passes the "refuse a capacity beyond a count it can hold" check and then `*value as usize` silently saturates to `usize::MAX`. The resulting cache never evicts, so the declared bound is not a bound at all. The tests exercise `1e300` (which is refused) but not the 2^64 boundary. Fix: compare against a properly representable bound, e.g. `*value < u64::MAX as f64` or reject anything `>= (usize::MAX as f64)` accounting for the rounding, so the rejection branch is actually reachable at the boundary.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
